{
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Se: one little word, many jobs",
      "body": "You've met several uses of *se*: reflexive (*se levanta*), the replacement for *le/les* (*se lo di*), and impersonal/passive (*se habla español*). Here are three more."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Reciprocal: each other",
      "body": "With plural subjects, *se / nos* can mean \"each other\": *Se quieren mucho.* (They love each other.) *Nos conocimos en la universidad.* (We met each other at university.) *Se escriben todos los días.* To avoid ambiguity with \"themselves\", add *el uno al otro*: *Se ayudan el uno al otro.*"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Accidental se: things that \"happen to\" you",
      "body": "For unplanned events — forgetting, dropping, breaking, losing — Spanish often makes the **thing** the subject and the person an indirect object, as if it happened to them:\n\n*se* + indirect pronoun (*me, te, le, nos, les*) + verb (agreeing with the thing):\n\n- *Se me olvidó.* (I forgot — literally \"it forgot itself on me\".)\n- *Se me olvidaron las llaves.* (I forgot the keys.)\n- *Se le cayó el vaso.* (He dropped the glass.)\n- *Se nos rompió la ventana.* (Our window broke.)\n- *Se me perdió el teléfono.* (I lost my phone.)\n- *Se te acabó el tiempo.* (You ran out of time.)\n- *Se me ocurrió una idea.* (An idea occurred to me.)\n\nIt softens responsibility — it was an accident!"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Passive se, review",
      "body": "*Se venden casas. Se habla inglés. Se necesita experiencia.* The verb agrees with the thing; nobody is named."
    }
  ],
